'''Zeuzera''' (zū-zē′rä̤), ''n.'' [NL. (Latreille, 1805): a corrupt form of unascertained origin.] A genus of bombycid moths, of the family ''Cossidæ'', or typical of a family ''Zeuzeridæ'', having the antennæ of the male unequally pectinate and bare at the tips. {{smaller|The genus has a wide distribution, and comprises about 30 species. ''Z. pyrina'', the wood-leopard, is common to Europe and the United States; its larva bores into the branches of the elm, maple, linden, ash, and many other trees.}}

