¶
'''Billet and zigzag.''' {{smaller|See ''billet''<sup>2</sup>.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Having sharp and quick turns or flexures; turning frequently back and forth; in ''bot.'', angularly bent from side to side.
{{smaller block|The road is steep and runs on ''zigzag'' terraces.
{{right|''Longfellow'', Hyperion, iii. 2.}}
I went through the ''zigzag'' passages [of a sap].
{{right|''J. K. Hosmer'', The Color-Guard, xiv.}}}}

'''Zigzag molding,''' {{smaller|in ''arch.'' See ''chevron'', 2, ''dancette'', 2.}}

'''zigzag''' (zig′zag), ''adv.'' [< ''zigzag'', ''a.''] In a zigzag manner; with frequent sharp turns.
{{smaller block|We patroled about, ''zig-zag'', as we could; the crowd … having no chief or regulator.
{{right|''Mme. D’Arblay'', Diary and Letters, IV. 235.}}
<poem>What you, Reader, and I
Would call going ''zig-zag''.</poem>
{{right|''Barham'', Ingoldsby Legends, II. 173.}}}}

'''zigzag''' (zig′zag), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''zigzagged'', ppr. ''zigzagging''. [< ''zigzag'', ''n.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' To move or advance in a zigzag fashion; form zigzags in a course; turn sharply back and forth.
{{smaller block|It was only by ''zigzagging'' in the most cautious manner … that we avoided getting floated altogether.
{{right|''O’Donovan'', Merv, xv.}}
<poem>Dread, uncanny thing,
With fuzzy breast and leathern wing;
In mad, ''zigzagging'' flight.</poem>
{{right|''J. W. Riley'', The Bat.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To form in zigzags, or with short turns or angles. ''T. Warton.''

'''zigzaggery''' (zig′zag-ėr-i), ''n.'' [< ''zigzag'' + ''-ery''.] The character of being zigzag; angular crookedness. [Rare.]

